## DESCRIPTION
## Matrix Algebra
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## Tagged by tda2d

## DBsubject(Linear algebra)
## DBchapter(Matrices)
## DBsection(Inverses)
## Institution(ASU)
## Level(2)
## TitleText1('Linear Algebra with Applications')
## AuthorText1('Jeffrey Holt')
## EditionText1('1')
## Section1('3.3')
## Problem1('')
## KEYWORDS('Algebra' 'Matrix' 'Matrices' 'Inverse')
DOCUMENT();        # This should be the first executable line in the problem.

loadMacros(
  "PGstandard.pl",
  "PGasu.pl",
  "PGchoicemacros.pl",
  "PGmatrixmacros.pl",
  "PGcourse.pl"
);

TEXT(beginproblem());
$showPartialCorrectAnswers = 1;

$a = non_zero_random(-6,6,1);
$b = non_zero_random(-5,5,1);
$c = non_zero_random(-4,4,1);
do {$d = random(-9,9,2);} until (($a * $d - $b * $c) != 0);

$B11 = non_zero_random(-3,3,1);
$B21 = non_zero_random(-3,3,1);

do {$B12 = non_zero_random(-3,3,1);} until ($B12 != $B11);
$B22 = non_zero_random(-4,4,1);

$det = $a * $d - $b * $c;

$ans11 = $d / $det;
$ans12 = - $b / $det;
$ans21 = - $c / $det;
$ans22 = $a / $det ;

$x1 = $ans11*$B11+$ans12*$B21;
$y1 = $ans21*$B11+$ans22*$B21;

$x2 = $ans11*$B12+$ans12*$B22;
$y2 = $ans21*$B12+$ans22*$B22;

$ls1 = nicestring([$a,$b],['x_1','x_2']);
$ls2 = nicestring([$c,$d],['x_1','x_2']);


BEGIN_TEXT
On this problem, you may use SAGE to check, but first compute by hand.  
Make sure you are using the exact inverse (will have fractions), not a decimal approximation.
$HR

Consider the following two systems:
$BCENTER
(a) 
\( \left\{"\{"\} \begin{array}{ccc}
$ls1 &=& $B11 \\
$ls2 &=& $B21
\end{array} \right. \)
\( \quad \) and \( \quad \)
(b)
\( \left\{"\{"\} \begin{array}{ccc}
$ls1 &=& $B12 \\
$ls2 &=& $B22
\end{array} \right.\)
$ECENTER
$HR

Find the inverse of the (common) coefficient matrix of the two systems.
$BR
$BCENTER
\{ mbox(
'\( A^{-1} = \)',
display_matrix([[ans_rule(10),ans_rule(10)],
[ans_rule(10),ans_rule(10)]],
'align'=>"cc")) \}
$ECENTER

$HR
Solve each system by using the inverse, i.e. by evaluating
\( A^{-1} \vec{b} \) where \( \vec{b} \) represents the right hand side.

$PAR That is,
\(\vec{b} = \left[ \begin{array}{r} $B11 \\ $B21 \end{array} \right] \) for  system (a) and
\(\vec{b} = \left[ \begin{array}{r} $B12 \\ $B22 \end{array} \right] \) for  system (b)).
$HR

Solution to  system (a) is  \( x_1  = \) \{ ans_rule(10)\} , \(x_2\) =\{ ans_rule(10) \}
$BR
Solution to system (b) is  \(x_1  =\) \{ans_rule(10)\} , \(x_2\) =\{ans_rule(10)\}

END_TEXT

ANS(num_cmp($ans11));
ANS(num_cmp($ans12));
ANS(num_cmp($ans21));
ANS(num_cmp($ans22));
ANS(num_cmp($x1));
ANS(num_cmp($y1));
ANS(num_cmp($x2));
ANS(num_cmp($y2));




ENDDOCUMENT();       # This should be the last executable line in the problem.